INSERT INTO repo_courses (course_id, title, domain, discipline, description, objectives, learning_outcomes, course_type, level, status) VALUES ('COURSE_051', 'Environmental Impact Assessment Law', 'Law', 'Law', 'Environmental Impact Assessment Law examines the legal architecture governing environmental assessment of proposed projects and plans. It covers statutory and regulatory bases, screening, scoping and terms of reference, impact assessment and appraisal, public participation, environmental decision making, post-approval compliance and monitoring, litigation, and comparative approaches.', 'Develop the ability to trace an EIA requirement through its legal basis, procedural stages, decision points, participation requirements, compliance obligations, and available remedies. Jurisdiction-specific legal rules should be attached as validated evidence rather than assumed in the core ontology.', 'Identify the legal basis and scope of EIA requirements; distinguish screening, scoping, assessment, appraisal, and decision stages; analyse participation and consultation requirements; evaluate compliance and monitoring obligations; and examine litigation and comparative approaches to EIA governance.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_052', 'Climate Change Law and Policy', 'Law', 'Law', 'Climate Change Law and Policy examines the legal and policy instruments used to govern mitigation, adaptation, climate finance, disclosure, accountability, and implementation. It connects domestic governance with international climate-law frameworks and considers how emissions regulation, resilience, finance, reporting, and litigation interact in policy implementation.', 'Develop the ability to distinguish legal obligations from policy commitments, analyse climate-governance instruments, and evaluate implementation and accountability mechanisms. Jurisdiction-specific legislation should be introduced only through validated legal sources.', 'Explain major climate-governance and legal frameworks; compare mitigation and adaptation instruments; analyse climate finance and disclosure mechanisms; interpret international climate-law arrangements; examine climate litigation and accountability; and evaluate implementation challenges across sectors and institutions.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_053', 'Contract Law and Commercial Agreements', 'Law', 'Law', 'Contract Law and Commercial Agreements develops the legal reasoning required to form, interpret, perform, manage, and resolve disputes arising from contracts. It covers formation, terms and interpretation, performance and discharge, breach and remedies, commercial risk allocation, drafting and negotiation, and common structures used in commercial agreements.', 'Develop the ability to identify contractual obligations and risk allocation, interpret contractual terms, assess performance and breach, and translate commercial requirements into legally coherent drafting and negotiation positions. Applicable jurisdictional rules should be validated separately.', 'Analyse formation and enforceability issues; interpret express and implied terms; assess performance, discharge, breach, and remedies; evaluate contractual allocation of commercial risk; identify drafting and negotiation issues; and analyse common commercial-agreement structures.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_054', 'Labour and Employment Law', 'Law', 'Law', 'Labour and Employment Law examines the legal regulation of employment relationships, working conditions, wages and benefits, industrial relations, workplace regulation, equality and workplace rights, dispute resolution, and contemporary employment issues. The course distinguishes individual employment obligations from collective labour relations and regulatory enforcement.', 'Develop the ability to identify employment-law issues, interpret workplace rights and obligations, analyse collective and individual disputes, and evaluate regulatory compliance. Jurisdiction-specific statutes and case law should be linked through validated legal sources.', 'Analyse employment relationships and conditions; evaluate wage, benefit, and workplace obligations; distinguish collective and individual labour relations; assess occupational and workplace regulation; analyse equality and workplace-rights issues; and evaluate dispute-resolution and adjudication mechanisms.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_055', 'Corporate, Sustainability and ESG Law', 'Law', 'Law', 'Corporate, Sustainability and ESG Law examines how corporate governance, directors'' duties, environmental and social responsibilities, disclosure, sustainable finance, and corporate risk are translated into legal obligations and accountability mechanisms. It treats ESG as a governance and legal domain rather than as a generic sustainability label.', 'Develop the ability to analyse corporate decision-making and accountability through governance, fiduciary, disclosure, sustainability, and risk lenses. Applicable corporate and securities-law requirements should be jurisdiction-specific and evidence-backed when implemented.', 'Explain corporate governance structures and directors'' duties; analyse environmental and social responsibilities; evaluate ESG disclosure and reporting obligations; assess sustainable-finance and corporate-risk issues; examine compliance and enforcement; and analyse ESG-related legal disputes and accountability mechanisms.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_056', 'Sociology', 'Social Science', 'Social Science', 'Sociology examines how social structures, institutions, culture, interaction, groups, organisations, inequality, population processes, and social change shape collective life. It introduces major sociological theories and applies them to empirical questions involving stratification, institutions, urbanisation, networks, and contemporary social transformations.', 'Develop sociological reasoning that distinguishes individual-level behaviour from structural and institutional processes. Emphasise theory-informed interpretation, evidence, comparative analysis, and awareness of how social context shapes observed outcomes.', 'Explain major sociological perspectives; analyse social institutions and processes of socialisation; examine culture, stratification, and inequality; analyse groups, organisations, and networks; interpret population and urbanisation patterns; and apply sociological theory to contemporary social change.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_057', 'Social Psychology', 'Social Science', 'Social Science', 'Social Psychology examines how people perceive, interpret, and influence one another and how social contexts shape cognition, attitudes, identity, behaviour, and group processes. It covers social cognition, attribution, attitudes and persuasion, interpersonal processes, groups and collective behaviour, identity and intergroup relations, social influence, communication, and applied social-psychological analysis.', 'Develop the ability to distinguish individual cognitive processes from interpersonal and group-level mechanisms and to interpret empirical evidence about social behaviour. Emphasise theory, experimental and correlational evidence, measurement, and contextual interpretation.', 'Explain core models of social cognition and attribution; analyse attitude formation and persuasion; evaluate interpersonal and group processes; examine identity and intergroup relations; analyse social influence and communication; and apply social-psychological theory to real-world problems.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_058', 'Development Economics', 'Social Science', 'Social Science', 'Development Economics examines economic processes associated with growth, structural transformation, poverty reduction, inequality, human capital, labour, institutions, markets, finance, public policy, and international integration. It combines economic models with empirical evidence to explain why development outcomes differ across people, regions, and countries.', 'Develop the ability to analyse development outcomes using economic theory and empirical evidence, while recognising institutional, distributional, and historical factors. Emphasise measurement, causal reasoning, policy evaluation, and trade-offs rather than treating development as a single growth metric.', 'Analyse growth and structural transformation; measure and interpret poverty and inequality; evaluate human-capital and labour-market mechanisms; examine institutions and development; analyse markets and development finance; evaluate development policies; and interpret international integration and development outcomes.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_059', 'Public Policy and Governance', 'Social Science', 'Social Science', 'Public Policy and Governance examines how public problems are defined, placed on policy agendas, translated into instruments, implemented through institutions, regulated, financed, and evaluated. It covers policy actors and institutions, formulation, implementation, governance and regulation, public finance, evaluation, digital governance, and contemporary policy challenges.', 'Develop the ability to trace policy from problem definition through formulation, implementation, and evaluation, while analysing institutional capacity, stakeholder interests, regulatory choices, resource constraints, and evidence.', 'Define and frame policy problems; analyse institutions and policy actors; evaluate policy-formulation alternatives; assess implementation mechanisms and institutional capacity; analyse governance and regulation; examine public-finance constraints; design evaluation approaches; and assess digital-governance developments.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active'), ('COURSE_060', 'Research Methods in Social Sciences', 'Social Science', 'Social Science', 'Research Methods in Social Sciences develops the methodological foundations for designing, conducting, analysing, and reporting empirical social-science research. It covers research questions and designs, sampling, measurement and instruments, qualitative methods, quantitative analysis, mixed-methods integration, validity and reliability, research quality, ethics, and research integrity.', 'Develop the ability to align research questions, theory, design, sampling, measurement, data collection, analysis, and inference. Emphasise methodological fit, transparency, validity, reliability, ethical conduct, and reproducibility.', 'Formulate researchable questions and hypotheses; select defensible research designs and sampling strategies; design and evaluate measurement instruments; apply qualitative and quantitative methods; integrate mixed-method evidence; assess validity and reliability; and conduct and report research in accordance with ethical and integrity requirements.', 'Core Technical', 'Suggested advanced undergraduate / postgraduate', 'Active') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;